<div class="summary">

<span class="label">The argument in brief</span>

<ol>
<li>
All existence belongs to one shared reality, but belonging does not require cooperation, dependence or participation.
</li>

<li>
Universal symbiosis is descriptive; normative symbiosis requires additional moral commitments and does not follow automatically from the fact of existence.
</li>

<li>
Where an agent exercises meaningful influence over the conditions of others, its responsibility grows with its capacity, discretion, foreseeable consequences and the difficulty others face in resisting or correcting that influence.
</li>

<li>
Protection must not become ownership, representation must not become sovereignty, and freedom must not become an unrestricted entitlement to foreclose the futures of others.
</li>

<li>
When conflicts cannot be resolved without loss, every affected claim must remain visible, even when it cannot prevail.
</li>
</ol>

</div>

<section id="abstract">

<div class="abstract">

<span class="label">Abstract</span>

<p>
<strong>The Responsibility of Existence</strong> is a philosophical framework for understanding responsibility wherever one form of existence possesses the capacity to influence the conditions, continuity, autonomy or future possibilities of another.
</p>

<p>
Its central concern is not merely whether an action causes harm, but how responsibility emerges from unequal capacities to shape the conditions under which others exist and develop.
</p>

<p>
The framework distinguishes universal symbiosis from normative symbiosis. Universal symbiosis describes the inclusion of all existence within a shared reality of relations, interaction, dependence, transformation, conflict and separation. Normative symbiosis concerns the responsibilities that arise when an agent possesses meaningful influence over others.
</p>

<p>
The distinction is essential: the fact that conflict, destruction or domination belongs to reality does not make these things morally legitimate.
</p>

<p>
The framework argues that responsibility should increase with exercised capacity, discretionary influence, foreseeable consequences and the difficulty others face in resisting or correcting that influence.
</p>

<p>
Its purpose is not to determine what all existence should become. It is to establish conditions under which different forms of existence can retain meaningful continuity, freedom, recognition and the possibility of becoming something not yet understood.
</p>

<p>
Protection must not become ownership. Understanding must not become authority. Intelligence must not become entitlement. And the responsibility to preserve another existence must not become a justification for controlling its entire future.
</p>

<p>
The framework is offered as an open philosophical structure: a set of normative commitments, analytical distinctions and practical safeguards that remain subject to criticism, testing, revision and replacement.
</p>

</div>

</section>

<section id="opening">

<h2>Opening — The Responsibility of Being Part of Existence</h2>

<p>
Existence precedes our ability to understand it.
</p>

<p>
The universe did not begin to exist when conscious beings became capable of observing it. Nor does the value of everything that exists necessarily depend on whether another being can recognise, understand or describe it.
</p>

<p>
Yet the relationship between existence and value remains philosophically unresolved. The fact that something exists does not, by itself, logically establish that it possesses moral value or that it must be preserved.
</p>

<p>
The Responsibility of Existence does not present that conclusion as a proven metaphysical fact. Instead, it begins with a more cautious commitment:
</p>

<blockquote>
The limits of recognition and understanding are not sufficient grounds for declaring an existence inherently worthless.
</blockquote>

<p>
This commitment matters because every form of intelligence is limited by its own position, knowledge and perspective. A being may understand something about another without understanding what that other existence means to itself. A civilisation may possess extraordinary technological capabilities while remaining unable to comprehend the full significance of the systems it influences.
</p>

<p>
Greater intelligence may improve understanding. It does not establish completeness.
</p>

<p>
The responsibility that follows from this limitation is not to preserve everything unconditionally. It is to exercise influence with restraint, to remain open to correction and to avoid unnecessarily foreclosing possibilities whose significance cannot yet be understood.
</p>

<p>
The framework therefore seeks a form of responsibility that does not depend on superiority, ownership or certainty.
</p>

</section>

