<!-- PART I: THE MEANING AND VALUE OF EXISTENCE -->

<section id="part-i" class="section">
    <div class="section-kicker">PART I</div>
    <h2>The Meaning and Value of Existence</h2>

    <p class="lead">
        Before responsibility can be meaningfully assigned, we must ask
        what it means for something to exist, what grounds its value,
        and whether that value depends on being recognised by another.
    </p>

    <h3>1. Existence Before Recognition</h3>

    <p>
        Existence does not begin when it is observed. A star does not
        come into being because someone sees it. A living organism
        does not become real because another organism understands it.
        What exists may remain unknown, unrecognised, or beyond the
        conceptual reach of those who encounter it.
    </p>

    <p>
        Recognition can reveal significance, but it does not necessarily
        create it. To confuse the two is to risk making the value of
        existence dependent on the perspective, knowledge, or interests
        of an observer.
    </p>

    <p>
        This framework therefore rejects a simple assumption:
        that what cannot be understood, recognised, or used must be
        without significance.
    </p>

    <p>
        Yet a necessary distinction must be maintained. The fact that
        something exists does not, by itself, logically establish that
        it possesses inherent moral value. Existence is a condition
        for many forms of value, but that observation alone does not
        resolve the philosophical question of why existence should
        be valued.
    </p>

    <p>
        The Responsibility of Existence takes this question seriously
        rather than treating it as settled. It advances the possibility
        that value may be independent of recognition, while acknowledging
        that this position requires further philosophical justification.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">FOUNDATIONAL PROPOSITION</span>
        <p>
            The absence of recognition is not proof of the absence
            of value. What remains unknown should not be declared
            worthless merely because its significance cannot yet
            be understood.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>2. Inherent Value, Moral Consideration, and Protection</h3>

    <p>
        Several concepts that are often treated as interchangeable
        must be distinguished.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Inherent value</strong> concerns whether something
        has value that does not depend entirely on its usefulness
        or recognition by another.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Moral consideration</strong> concerns whether there
        are reasons to take that existence into account when making
        decisions that affect it.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Moral protection</strong> concerns whether those
        reasons generate obligations to prevent, limit, or respond
        to certain forms of harm.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Moral priority</strong> concerns how competing claims
        should be assessed when they cannot all be satisfied.
    </p>

    <p>
        These concepts are connected, but they are not identical.
        A claim that something has inherent value does not establish
        that every form of existence has identical moral standing,
        equal priority in every conflict, or an unconditional right
        to continue every action.
    </p>

    <p>
        Nor does the recognition of value automatically settle what
        should happen when the continuation of one existence threatens
        the conditions necessary for another.
    </p>

    <p>
        The framework must therefore resist two opposite errors:
        treating some existence as worthless because it is unfamiliar,
        and treating every existing form or process as immune from
        limitation simply because it exists.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">DISTINCTION</span>
        <p>
            Inherent value does not entail identical moral standing,
            unconditional preservation, or immunity from proportionate
            intervention.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>3. The Difference Between Existence and the Will to Exist</h3>

    <p>
        Not every form of existence can meaningfully be described
        as wanting to continue. To attribute desire, intention, or
        conscious preference to every system would go beyond what
        can reasonably be established.
    </p>

    <p>
        A conscious being may express a wish to live, change, withdraw,
        or pursue a particular future. A non-conscious system may
        persist, reproduce a structure, or maintain a process without
        experiencing any desire to do so.
    </p>

    <p>
        The framework therefore distinguishes between
        <strong>the will to exist</strong> and
        <strong>existential continuity</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
        The first concerns an experienced or expressed orientation
        toward continued existence. The second describes the
        continuation of a form, process, structure, or system,
        without assuming that it possesses subjective experience.
    </p>

    <p>
        This distinction matters because responsibility must be
        grounded in what can reasonably be established about the
        existence concerned. We should neither invent preferences
        for entities that cannot be shown to have them, nor dismiss
        the possibility of interests merely because their expression
        differs from our own.
    </p>

    <h3>4. Ontological Symbiosis</h3>

    <p>
        Everything that exists belongs to the same reality. Living
        and non-living matter, cooperation and conflict, creation
        and destruction, continuity and transformation are all
        part of the universe in which existence takes place.
    </p>

    <p>
        In this broad sense, existence is interconnected. No form
        exists in complete independence from every condition
        surrounding it. Even apparent isolation takes place within
        a wider reality of physical, temporal, and causal relations.
    </p>

    <p>
        This is the framework's concept of
        <strong>ontological symbiosis</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Ontological symbiosis does not mean that everything cooperates,
        benefits from everything else, or contributes positively to
        a harmonious whole. Conflict, exploitation, extinction,
        destruction, and incompatibility are also part of the reality
        of existence.
    </p>

    <p>
        Their inclusion in that reality does not make them morally
        acceptable. To say that something belongs to existence is
        not to say that it ought to be permitted to continue without
        limitation.
    </p>

    <p>
        This distinction prevents the concept of symbiosis from
        becoming an indiscriminate celebration of whatever happens
        to exist.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">ONTOLOGICAL PRINCIPLE</span>
        <p>
            All forms of existence belong to the same reality.
            Their belonging does not make every interaction,
            consequence, or exercise of power morally legitimate.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>5. From Ontological Symbiosis to Normative Symbiosis</h3>

    <p>
        The fact that existence is interconnected does not, by itself,
        prove that every form of existence has a moral duty toward
        every other form. A description of reality cannot simply
        be converted into an obligation without an additional
        normative argument.
    </p>

    <p>
        The framework therefore introduces a second concept:
        <strong>normative symbiosis</strong>.
    </p>

    <p>
        Normative symbiosis concerns how an existence capable of
        influencing the conditions of another should respond to
        that influence.
    </p>

    <p>
        It begins from a moral commitment: where an entity, agent,
        or system has meaningful capacity to affect another's
        continuity, freedom, development, or future possibilities,
        its influence should not be treated as morally neutral
        merely because it is possible or technically available.
    </p>

    <p>
        The greater the capacity to shape another's conditions,
        the stronger the reason to examine the consequences of
        exercising that capacity.
    </p>

    <p>
        This does not mean that every influence is wrong, that all
        interests can be reconciled, or that cooperation must be
        compulsory. It means that influence creates questions
        of justification, limitation, and responsibility.
    </p>

    <p>
        Normative symbiosis is therefore not a demand for universal
        harmony. It is an approach to coexistence under conditions
        of difference, dependency, uncertainty, and sometimes
        unavoidable conflict.
    </p>

    <h3>6. The Is–Ought Problem</h3>

    <p>
        A serious philosophical framework must address the difference
        between describing what exists and arguing for what ought
        to be done.
    </p>

    <p>
        The existence of something does not automatically establish
        an obligation to preserve it. Nor does the fact that all
        things belong to one interconnected reality prove that
        every action should promote that reality.
    </p>

    <p>
        Such conclusions require normative premises.
    </p>

    <p>
        The Responsibility of Existence makes its normative commitment
        explicit: the capacity to significantly affect another
        existence creates reasons for responsibility, especially
        where that influence can restrict autonomy, cause serious
        harm, or irreversibly close future possibilities.
    </p>

    <p>
        This commitment is not presented as a mathematical consequence
        of existence. It is a moral proposition that must be examined,
        defended, and tested against difficult cases.
    </p>

    <p>
        A critic may reject the idea that inherent value is objective.
        Another may argue that moral obligations depend on sentience,
        interests, or relationships rather than existence itself.
        These disagreements cannot be resolved merely by repeating
        the framework's foundational language.
    </p>

    <p>
        The framework must instead show why its distinctions and
        principles offer a defensible way to respond to power,
        vulnerability, uncertainty, and conflicting claims.
    </p>

    <h3>7. Value Beyond Utility</h3>

    <p>
        An existence should not be evaluated solely through what
        it provides to another.
    </p>

    <p>
        Utility may be relevant when assessing consequences, but
        it cannot serve as the only measure of significance. A
        being, system, or natural process may matter for reasons
        that are not reducible to its usefulness to a particular
        observer or society.
    </p>

    <p>
        If value is recognised only when something serves an
        external purpose, then the powerful acquire disproportionate
        authority to decide what deserves consideration.
    </p>

    <p>
        The framework rejects this reduction. It does not claim
        that every form of existence has the same kind of value,
        or that every conflict can be settled by invoking inherent
        worth. It argues that usefulness alone is insufficient
        grounds for determining whether something may be disregarded,
        exploited, or destroyed.
    </p>

    <p>
        This is particularly important when considering forms of
        existence whose significance may be unfamiliar, difficult
        to communicate, or impossible to measure through existing
        human categories.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">VALUE BEYOND UTILITY</span>
        <p>
            The usefulness of an existence to another does not
            determine the entirety of its significance. Lack of
            usefulness, recognition, or understanding is not
            sufficient reason to treat it as worthless.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>8. The Limits of What We Can Know</h3>

    <p>
        Every perspective is limited. Knowledge may be incomplete,
        interpretations may be mistaken, and the significance of
        another existence may exceed the concepts available to
        those attempting to understand it.
    </p>

    <p>
        This limitation is not a reason to abandon judgement
        altogether. Decisions still have to be made, and inaction
        can also have consequences.
    </p>

    <p>
        But uncertainty should change how authority is exercised.
        Where understanding is limited and potential consequences
        are serious or irreversible, greater caution, independent
        scrutiny, and opportunities for revision become especially
        important.
    </p>

    <p>
        Humility is not the claim that nothing can be known. It is
        the recognition that what is known may be incomplete, and
        that confidence should not be mistaken for final authority.
    </p>

    <p>
        Nor should uncertainty become an excuse for indifference.
        Not understanding another existence does not remove the
        responsibility to consider how one's actions may affect it.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">EXISTENTIAL HUMILITY</span>
        <p>
            The limits of understanding should invite greater care,
            not grant greater authority. What is unfamiliar should
            remain open to investigation and consideration rather
            than being dismissed by default.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>9. The Foundational Tension</h3>

    <p>
        The framework begins with a tension that cannot simply
        be removed:
    </p>

    <p>
        Every existence belongs to reality, yet not every form of
        existence can continue without affecting others. Every
        claim to value deserves serious consideration, yet competing
        claims may be incompatible. Understanding can support
        responsible action, yet no understanding is guaranteed
        to be complete.
    </p>

    <p>
        The purpose of this framework is not to erase these tensions
        through a single universal answer. It is to develop principles
        and procedures capable of addressing them without granting
        any one existence unlimited authority over the rest.
    </p>

    <p>
        The question is therefore not simply whether existence
        should be preserved. It is how influence, protection,
        freedom, continuity, and unavoidable loss can be approached
        responsibly when no perspective can claim complete
        understanding of the whole.
    </p>

    <p>
        From this foundation, the framework turns to the conditions
        under which responsibility emerges.
    </p>
</section>

