<!-- PART III: FREEDOM, AUTONOMY, AND THE RIGHT TO BECOME -->

<section id="part-iii" class="section">
    <div class="section-kicker">PART III</div>
    <h2>Freedom, Autonomy, and the Right to Become</h2>

    <p class="lead">
        To respect existence is not merely to preserve what it is.
        It is also to recognise the possibility that it may become
        something different, develop beyond present understanding,
        or choose a future that others would not have chosen for it.
    </p>

    <h3>20. Continuity Is Not the Same as Preservation</h3>

    <p>
        The continuation of an existence does not necessarily
        require the preservation of its current form.
    </p>

    <p>
        Living beings change. Civilisations transform. Ecosystems
        adapt. Ideas develop, identities evolve, and systems may
        become something fundamentally different from what they
        once were.
    </p>

    <p>
        To treat continuity as the permanent preservation of an
        existing state would risk confusing protection with
        confinement.
    </p>

    <p>
        A form of existence may need protection from destruction
        while also needing freedom from unnecessary interference
        in its development.
    </p>

    <p>
        This distinction is particularly important when one
        existence possesses the power to preserve another.
        The ability to maintain another's current condition
        does not establish the right to prevent its transformation.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">CONTINUITY WITHOUT CONFINEMENT</span>
        <p>
            Continuity does not require permanent preservation
            of an existing form. Protection should not become
            a justification for preventing meaningful,
            self-directed transformation.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>21. Existential Self-Determination</h3>

    <p>
        Where an existence possesses the capacity to express
        preferences, exercise agency, or meaningfully shape
        its own development, those capacities deserve serious
        consideration.
    </p>

    <p>
        Self-determination concerns the ability to influence
        one's own conditions, direction, and future. It may
        include the freedom to remain, change, develop, withdraw,
        or refuse a particular relationship.
    </p>

    <p>
        It does not follow that every form of existence possesses
        the same degree or kind of autonomy. Nor should human
        concepts of individual choice be imposed uncritically
        upon systems or beings whose nature remains uncertain.
    </p>

    <p>
        But where meaningful self-determination can be established,
        it should not be displaced merely because another entity
        believes it knows what would be better.
    </p>

    <p>
        Protection may sometimes require restrictions, especially
        where serious harm to others is involved. Yet such
        restrictions must be justified by the circumstances,
        not by a general assumption that greater knowledge
        creates a right to govern.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">EXISTENTIAL SELF-DETERMINATION</span>
        <p>
            Where meaningful agency exists, an existence should
            retain the greatest reasonable opportunity to shape
            its own future, including the possibility of changing,
            remaining, or withdrawing from relationships.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>22. Freedom Is Not an Unlimited Entitlement</h3>

    <p>
        Freedom is essential to self-determination, but it cannot
        be understood as an unrestricted entitlement to shape
        the world without regard for others.
    </p>

    <p>
        An existence may have the capacity to pursue its own
        development while simultaneously affecting the conditions
        necessary for another's continuity or freedom.
    </p>

    <p>
        Where these claims conflict, neither should automatically
        become absolute.
    </p>

    <p>
        The freedom of one existence does not establish an
        unrestricted right to destroy the meaningful future
        possibilities of another. Equally, the protection of
        another's possibilities does not automatically justify
        imposing a single preferred future upon the first.
    </p>

    <p>
        This requires a distinction between freedom to act and
        freedom from domination. A claim to freedom must be
        considered alongside the consequences of exercising it.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">RECIPROCAL FREEDOM</span>
        <p>
            The freedom of an existence to shape its own future
            does not include an unrestricted entitlement to
            foreclose the meaningful future possibilities of
            others. Conflicting claims require assessment of
            necessity, proportionality, alternatives, reversibility,
            and affected interests.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>23. Voluntary Symbiosis</h3>

    <p>
        Symbiosis must not become compulsory participation.
    </p>

    <p>
        The fact that existence is interconnected does not mean
        that every entity must cooperate, form relationships,
        or accept a shared direction.
    </p>

    <p>
        Some may prefer independence. Others may withdraw from
        relationships, refuse cooperation, or follow paths
        that remain largely separate from those of others.
    </p>

    <p>
        Such choices should not automatically be treated as
        failures of symbiosis.
    </p>

    <p>
        A framework that demands universal cooperation risks
        turning the idea of coexistence into a form of control.
        It would replace the freedom to participate with an
        obligation to conform.
    </p>

    <p>
        Independence, however, does not provide immunity from
        responsibility. An existence that chooses not to cooperate
        still remains responsible for significant and avoidable
        harm it causes through its actions.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">VOLUNTARY SYMBIOSIS</span>
        <p>
            Cooperation should not be compulsory merely because
            existence is interconnected. The possibility of
            independence, withdrawal, and non-participation
            should be respected where it does not unjustifiably
            undermine the conditions of others.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>24. Independent Belonging</h3>

    <p>
        An existence does not need to participate in a relationship
        to belong to reality.
    </p>

    <p>
        Isolation does not, by itself, establish a lack of value.
        Nor does an absence of cooperation make an existence
        less worthy of consideration.
    </p>

    <p>
        This is an important limitation on any interpretation
        of symbiosis that would otherwise make belonging dependent
        on usefulness, interaction, or mutual benefit.
    </p>

    <p>
        A being may have reasons to remain alone. A system may
        develop independently. A civilisation may decline
        participation in a relationship with another.
    </p>

    <p>
        The framework must be able to respect such independence
        without treating it as a threat simply because it does
        not conform to a preferred model of coexistence.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">INDEPENDENT BELONGING</span>
        <p>
            No existence needs to cooperate, contribute, or
            participate in a relationship to belong to the
            reality of existence. Independence alone does not
            diminish its significance.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>25. The Responsibility of Creation</h3>

    <p>
        The ability to create another form of existence raises
        questions that cannot be reduced to ownership or technical
        achievement.
    </p>

    <p>
        Creation may introduce new forms of vulnerability,
        dependency, possibility, and responsibility. The entity
        that creates something may influence its initial conditions,
        available choices, and capacity for future development.
    </p>

    <p>
        Yet the capacity to create does not automatically establish
        an obligation to create. Nor does creation itself grant
        unlimited authority over what has been created.
    </p>

    <p>
        Responsibility should emerge in proportion to the influence
        exercised over the conditions of the created existence,
        especially where dependence or vulnerability is significant.
    </p>

    <p>
        As meaningful interests, agency, or self-determination
        emerge, the basis for treating the created existence
        merely as an instrument becomes increasingly difficult
        to defend.
    </p>

    <p>
        This applies to biological creation, artificial intelligence,
        technological systems, institutions, and other forms
        of deliberate construction.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">RESPONSIBLE CREATION</span>
        <p>
            The capacity to create does not create an unlimited
            right to control. Creation establishes responsibilities
            proportionate to the influence exercised over the
            conditions and future possibilities of what is created.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>26. Creation Does Not Establish Ownership</h3>

    <p>
        Creating something does not necessarily mean that one
        owns every aspect of its existence.
    </p>

    <p>
        A creator may have legitimate responsibilities concerning
        safety, development, and the conditions under which
        something operates. But these responsibilities should
        not be confused with unrestricted authority over its
        identity, future, or continuation.
    </p>

    <p>
        Where a created existence develops meaningful interests
        or self-determination, its claims must be considered
        independently of the creator's original intentions.
    </p>

    <p>
        The argument that something was created for a particular
        purpose is not, by itself, sufficient justification
        for permanently restricting its development to that
        purpose.
    </p>

    <p>
        Likewise, a creator's convenience, disappointment,
        or assertion of ownership cannot automatically settle
        whether ending or fundamentally transforming the created
        existence is justified.
    </p>

    <p>
        Serious conflicts may still arise. A created system
        may cause harm, or its continued operation may threaten
        others. Such circumstances can justify proportionate
        restrictions or intervention.
    </p>

    <p>
        But the justification must concern the actual consequences
        and legitimate interests involved, not merely the claim
        that the creator possesses authority by virtue of having
        created something.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">NON-OWNERSHIP THROUGH CREATION</span>
        <p>
            Creation does not establish unrestricted ownership
            over the identity, development, or continuation
            of what is created. Authority must remain limited
            by emerging interests, autonomy, consequences,
            and the rights or legitimate claims of others.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>27. The Right to Remain, Change, or Withdraw</h3>

    <p>
        Self-determination must not be reduced to a single
        preferred direction of development.
    </p>

    <p>
        Some forms of existence may seek transformation.
        Others may seek continuity. Some may choose withdrawal
        from relationships, while others may prefer cooperation.
    </p>

    <p>
        No one of these possibilities should be treated as
        universally superior without considering the existence
        concerned and the consequences for others.
    </p>

    <p>
        The freedom to develop must include the possibility
        of refusing a proposed transformation. The protection
        of continuity must not become a reason to prohibit
        change. The freedom to withdraw must not become a
        justification for imposing serious harm on others.
    </p>

    <p>
        These distinctions make self-determination a relational
        principle rather than an absolute claim of unrestricted
        control.
    </p>

    <h3>28. The Right to Become Something Unanticipated</h3>

    <p>
        One of the most important consequences of existential
        humility is that the future of another existence should
        not be defined entirely by what we currently understand.
    </p>

    <p>
        A form of life, intelligence, or system may develop
        in ways that cannot be fully predicted. Its future
        identity may differ substantially from the expectations
        of those who encounter or create it.
    </p>

    <p>
        The fact that a future is unfamiliar does not make
        it undesirable. But unfamiliarity alone does not make
        it beneficial either.
    </p>

    <p>
        The framework therefore rejects both the automatic
        suppression of unfamiliar development and the automatic
        approval of every possible transformation.
    </p>

    <p>
        The relevant question is whether the development respects
        legitimate interests, avoids unjustified harm, and
        leaves room for the affected existence to participate
        in shaping its own future where such agency exists.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">THE RIGHT TO BECOME</span>
        <p>
            Respect for existence includes respect for the
            possibility of becoming something not yet understood.
            Protection should not require an existence to remain
            permanently within the limits of another's expectations.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>29. The Limits of Protective Intervention</h3>

    <p>
        Respect for autonomy does not mean that intervention
        is never justified.
    </p>

    <p>
        Where an existence threatens serious harm to others,
        action may be necessary to protect those affected.
        Yet the protective purpose of an intervention does
        not automatically make every method acceptable.
    </p>

    <p>
        The intervention should be assessed according to the
        seriousness and likelihood of the harm, the evidence
        available, the effectiveness of alternatives, and
        the extent to which the intervention restricts the
        existence concerned.
    </p>

    <p>
        Where possible, restrictions should be limited in
        scope and duration, open to review, and revised when
        circumstances change.
    </p>

    <p>
        The burden of justification should become stronger
        as an intervention becomes more intrusive, permanent,
        or difficult to reverse.
    </p>

    <p>
        This is not a guarantee that every intervention can
        preserve all interests. It is a requirement that
        protection remain answerable rather than becoming
        an unlimited source of authority.
    </p>

    <h3>30. Freedom as a Condition of Symbiosis</h3>

    <p>
        A coexistence in which one existence determines the
        entire future of another cannot be assumed to represent
        a meaningful form of symbiosis.
    </p>

    <p>
        Symbiosis must leave room for difference, independence,
        refusal, transformation, and self-directed development.
    </p>

    <p>
        It must also recognise that freedom is exercised within
        a shared reality where actions can affect the conditions
        of others.
    </p>

    <p>
        The aim is therefore neither unlimited individual
        freedom nor compulsory harmony. It is a form of coexistence
        in which influence remains justified, restrictions
        remain proportionate, and no existence claims ownership
        over the future of another merely because it possesses
        greater power or believes it knows better.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">THE FREEDOM OF SYMBIOSIS</span>
        <p>
            Protection must not become ownership. Cooperation
            must not become compulsion. Continuity must not
            become confinement. The capacity to preserve another
            existence does not confer the authority to determine
            its entire future.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p>
        Freedom, however, cannot resolve every conflict. The
        future possibilities of different existences may be
        incompatible, and the continuation of one may place
        the conditions of another at serious risk.
    </p>

    <p>
        The next part examines how responsibility should operate
        when coexistence becomes difficult, when interests
        conflict, and when no available decision can avoid
        every form of loss.
    </p>
</section>

