<!-- PART IV: CONFLICT, LIMITS, AND THE CONDITIONS OF COEXISTENCE -->

<section id="part-iv" class="section">
    <div class="section-kicker">PART IV</div>
    <h2>Conflict, Limits, and the Conditions of Coexistence</h2>

    <p class="lead">
        Symbios cannot be tested only through situations in which
        coexistence is easy. Its principles must also remain
        meaningful when interests conflict, when influence becomes
        destructive, and when the continuation of different
        existences cannot be fully reconciled.
    </p>

    <h3>31. Symbiosis Does Not Require Harmony</h3>

    <p>
        Existence is not characterised by universal agreement.
        Conflict, competition, dependency, and incompatible
        interests are recurring features of reality.
    </p>

    <p>
        A framework for symbiosis must therefore be able to
        address conflict without assuming that every disagreement
        can or should be eliminated.
    </p>

    <p>
        Difference alone does not establish a justification
        for domination or destruction. Nor does the presence
        of conflict automatically mean that coexistence has
        failed.
    </p>

    <p>
        Two existences may pursue different directions, maintain
        independent identities, or reject one another's values
        while still recognising limits on how they exercise
        influence.
    </p>

    <p>
        The objective is not to create uniformity. It is to
        establish conditions under which difference can persist
        without one existence acquiring unrestricted authority
        over the other.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">SYMBIOTIC COEXISTENCE</span>
        <p>
            Symbiosis does not require uniformity, agreement,
            or the absence of conflict. Difference alone does
            not justify domination or destruction. Where
            possible, coexistence should preserve independent
            continuity and meaningful development.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>32. The Individual and the Whole</h3>

    <p>
        One of the most difficult questions concerns the relationship
        between individual existence and the wider systems
        on which existence depends.
    </p>

    <p>
        An individual may depend on an ecosystem, community,
        or larger structure for its continued existence.
        At the same time, the preservation of that larger
        structure may sometimes conflict with the interests
        of particular individuals within it.
    </p>

    <p>
        Neither the individual nor the whole should automatically
        receive absolute priority.
    </p>

    <p>
        Treating the whole as supreme can justify sacrificing
        individuals whenever their interests are inconvenient.
        Treating every individual claim as absolute can, in
        turn, undermine the conditions required for other
        individuals and systems to continue.
    </p>

    <p>
        The framework therefore rejects both unrestricted
        collective sacrifice and unrestricted individual
        entitlement.
    </p>

    <p>
        Decisions must consider the actual interests at stake,
        the severity of potential harm, the availability of
        alternatives, and the consequences of preserving
        one condition at the expense of another.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">RECIPROCAL EXISTENTIAL CONSIDERATION</span>
        <p>
            Neither an individual existence nor the wider whole
            automatically possesses absolute priority. Decisions
            must consider both the interests of particular
            existences and the conditions that make continued
            coexistence possible.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>33. The Conditions of Continued Existence</h3>

    <p>
        Every form of existence depends on conditions that
        make its continuation possible.
    </p>

    <p>
        These conditions may be biological, ecological, physical,
        social, informational, or technological. Some may be
        visible and well understood. Others may be complex,
        indirect, or only partially known.
    </p>

    <p>
        The destruction of these conditions may affect far
        more than the immediate target of an action.
    </p>

    <p>
        A decision that appears beneficial to one individual
        or institution may undermine the wider conditions
        upon which many other existences depend.
    </p>

    <p>
        Responsibility must therefore extend beyond immediate
        outcomes. It should also consider dependencies, indirect
        effects, long-term consequences, and the resilience
        of the systems involved.
    </p>

    <p>
        However, the existence of a larger system does not
        automatically justify every sacrifice demanded in
        its name. Systemic importance must be examined rather
        than assumed.
    </p>

    <h3>34. Incompatible Continuity</h3>

    <p>
        Some conflicts cannot be resolved through compromise.
    </p>

    <p>
        There may be situations in which the continued operation
        or development of one existence directly threatens
        the conditions necessary for another to continue.
        The conflict may be genuine even when neither party
        seeks it.
    </p>

    <p>
        In such cases, the framework cannot simply demand
        that everything be preserved. That would avoid the
        actual problem rather than address it.
    </p>

    <p>
        Nor should the existence with greater power automatically
        determine which continuation matters most.
    </p>

    <p>
        The assessment must examine whether the conflict is
        unavoidable, whether less destructive alternatives
        exist, how severe and foreseeable the consequences
        are, and whether the proposed response is proportionate.
    </p>

    <p>
        Where a loss cannot be avoided, the existence affected
        must not be treated as expendable merely because
        its continuation is incompatible with another.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">INCOMPATIBLE CONTINUITY</span>
        <p>
            Where legitimate conditions for different forms
            of existence cannot be reconciled, decisions must
            be guided by necessity, proportionality, available
            alternatives, reversibility, and fair consideration
            of affected interests. Unavoidable loss does not
            make the affected existence morally meaningless.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>35. The Limits of Preservation</h3>

    <p>
        Preservation is not an absolute objective.
    </p>

    <p>
        A demand to preserve every existing condition regardless
        of consequences could prevent necessary change, maintain
        destructive arrangements, or expose others to avoidable
        harm.
    </p>

    <p>
        Some processes need to end. Some structures need to
        change. Some forms of influence must be restricted.
    </p>

    <p>
        The question is not whether an existing condition
        must always continue, but whether ending or transforming
        it can be justified in relation to the legitimate
        interests and conditions affected.
    </p>

    <p>
        A distinction must also be maintained between ending
        a harmful action and eliminating the existence responsible
        for it. Restricting conduct, changing conditions,
        or limiting access may sometimes address a threat
        without requiring the most destructive available response.
    </p>

    <p>
        Where more serious intervention is genuinely necessary,
        the burden of justification must increase accordingly.
    </p>

    <h3>36. The Principle of Proportionality</h3>

    <p>
        Proportionality is necessary because legitimate protection
        can itself become a source of harm.
    </p>

    <p>
        An intervention should not impose restrictions or
        consequences substantially greater than those reasonably
        required to address the threat.
    </p>

    <p>
        Its justification should consider at least five questions:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li>Is the harm sufficiently serious and supported by credible evidence?</li>
        <li>Is intervention genuinely necessary to address it?</li>
        <li>Are less restrictive alternatives available?</li>
        <li>Are the likely consequences proportionate to the threat?</li>
        <li>Can the decision be independently reviewed and revised?</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        Proportionality does not mean that every affected interest
        can be preserved. It means that the response must be
        justified by the actual circumstances rather than
        by fear, convenience, or an unlimited claim of authority.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">PROPORTIONATE INTERVENTION</span>
        <p>
            Intervention should be no more restrictive or
            destructive than reasonably necessary to address
            a legitimate and sufficiently serious concern.
            Its scope should remain open to review and revision
            where possible.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>37. The Principle of Reversibility</h3>

    <p>
        Decisions made under uncertainty should preserve the
        possibility of correction wherever reasonably possible.
    </p>

    <p>
        Reversibility does not mean that every action can
        be undone. Some consequences are permanent, and some
        decisions must be made within limited time.
    </p>

    <p>
        It does mean that irreversible actions deserve a
        stronger burden of justification, particularly when
        the affected existence cannot meaningfully participate
        in the decision.
    </p>

    <p>
        Where uncertainty is substantial, temporary, limited,
        and reviewable measures may be preferable to permanent
        interventions, provided that they adequately address
        the risks involved.
    </p>

    <p>
        Reversibility is not an excuse for delaying necessary
        action indefinitely. It is a way to reduce the cost
        of mistakes when the consequences of being wrong
        may be severe.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">REVERSIBILITY</span>
        <p>
            Where uncertainty is significant, preserve the
            possibility of correction wherever reasonably
            possible. The more irreversible the consequence,
            the stronger the required justification.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>38. The Responsibility to Adapt</h3>

    <p>
        Coexistence cannot depend entirely on one existence
        adapting to the demands of another.
    </p>

    <p>
        When the development or continuation of one form
        of existence restricts the conditions of another,
        the first question should not automatically be how
        the weaker or less powerful existence can change.
    </p>

    <p>
        The distribution of responsibility must take account
        of who possesses the capacity to adapt, who controls
        the relevant conditions, and who would bear the costs
        of change.
    </p>

    <p>
        An entity with greater influence may have greater
        practical capacity to modify its own conduct or
        environment. That capacity can create a stronger
        reason to consider adaptation on its part.
    </p>

    <p>
        This does not mean that the more powerful must always
        change, or that every demand made by a less powerful
        existence is automatically justified.
    </p>

    <p>
        It means that the burden of adaptation should not
        be placed on the less powerful merely because doing
        so is easier for those who control the situation.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">RECIPROCAL ADAPTATION</span>
        <p>
            Where coexistence requires change, responsibility
            for adaptation should be assessed according to
            influence, control, capacity, consequences, and
            the interests affected. Power alone should not
            determine who must bear the burden.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>39. The Protection of Future Possibilities</h3>

    <p>
        Responsibility does not end with the immediate present.
        Actions taken today may determine which forms of existence
        can emerge, develop, or continue in the future.
    </p>

    <p>
        The destruction of habitats, irreversible technological
        decisions, permanent restrictions on development,
        and the elimination of independent possibilities
        may affect entities that cannot yet participate in
        present decisions.
    </p>

    <p>
        Future possibilities therefore deserve consideration
        when present influence can permanently determine
        the conditions under which they may arise.
    </p>

    <p>
        This does not establish a duty to create every possible
        future existence. Nor does it mean that hypothetical
        future interests automatically override the legitimate
        interests of those who exist now.
    </p>

    <p>
        It means that avoidable and irreversible foreclosure
        of meaningful possibilities requires justification.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">RESPONSIBILITY TOWARD FUTURE POSSIBILITIES</span>
        <p>
            Those capable of irreversibly shaping future
            conditions should consider the possibilities
            their actions preserve or eliminate. Unnecessary
            destruction of meaningful future opportunities
            requires justification.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>40. Future Plurality</h3>

    <p>
        The future should not be understood as a single destination
        that present power is entitled to determine.
    </p>

    <p>
        Different forms of existence may develop in different
        directions. Their values, structures, and ways of
        relating to reality may remain distinct.
    </p>

    <p>
        A future in which only one preferred form of existence
        is permitted to develop risks turning protection
        into a project of uniformity.
    </p>

    <p>
        The framework therefore recognises the importance
        of preserving a plurality of meaningful possibilities:
        continuity, transformation, diversity, independence,
        and different forms of coexistence.
    </p>

    <p>
        This is not an argument that the greatest possible
        number of futures must always be maximised. Some
        possibilities may depend on destructive conditions,
        and not every imaginable future represents a legitimate
        interest.
    </p>

    <p>
        Future plurality must remain subject to the same
        principles of responsibility, proportionality, and
        consideration that apply to present existence.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">FUTURE PLURALITY</span>
        <p>
            Where reasonably possible, preserve a diversity
            of meaningful future possibilities rather than
            imposing one preferred direction of development.
            Plurality is a consideration, not an absolute
            demand to preserve every imaginable possibility.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>41. The No-Final-Vantage Principle</h3>

    <p>
        No individual, institution, civilisation, or intelligence
        should be assumed to possess a complete and unquestionable
        understanding of existence.
    </p>

    <p>
        Greater intelligence may expand the ability to understand
        complex systems. Greater knowledge may improve prediction.
        Greater power may increase the capacity to intervene.
    </p>

    <p>
        None of these qualities establishes a final perspective
        from which every other existence can be judged without
        the possibility of error.
    </p>

    <p>
        This principle applies equally to authorities that
        claim to represent the interests of humanity, nature,
        future generations, or symbiosis itself.
    </p>

    <p>
        Such authorities may have legitimate roles, but their
        decisions must remain open to scrutiny, challenge,
        and revision.
    </p>

    <p>
        The framework must not reproduce the very concentration
        of authority it seeks to limit.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">NO FINAL VANTAGE</span>
        <p>
            No existence should claim unquestionable final
            authority over the value, interests, or future
            of others solely on the basis of intelligence,
            knowledge, power, or certainty.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>42. No Judge in One's Own Cause</h3>

    <p>
        An entity exercising significant influence should
        not be the only authority deciding whether that influence
        was justified.
    </p>

    <p>
        This is especially important where the entity has
        an interest in the outcome, controls the available
        information, or possesses the power to exclude those
        affected from meaningful participation.
    </p>

    <p>
        Independent scrutiny can help expose conflicts of
        interest, overlooked consequences, distorted assumptions,
        and unjustified restrictions.
    </p>

    <p>
        Independence does not guarantee correctness. Reviewers
        may also be mistaken, biased, or limited in their
        understanding. They must therefore remain subject
        to appropriate scrutiny themselves.
    </p>

    <p>
        The objective is not to create an infallible judge.
        It is to prevent any single actor from possessing
        both unrestricted power and unquestionable authority
        over the assessment of its own conduct.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">NO JUDGE IN ONE'S OWN CAUSE</span>
        <p>
            Where significant influence is exercised, those
            responsible should not possess exclusive authority
            to determine whether their own decisions were
            justified. Independent review and meaningful
            contestation should be available wherever feasible.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>43. The Limits of the Greater Good</h3>

    <p>
        Appeals to collective benefit can sometimes justify
        decisions that impose severe and unequal costs on
        particular existences.
    </p>

    <p>
        A decision may produce substantial benefits for many
        while permanently removing the conditions necessary
        for a smaller group to continue.
    </p>

    <p>
        Aggregate benefit is relevant to moral assessment,
        but it cannot automatically settle every conflict.
    </p>

    <p>
        The framework rejects the assumption that a sufficiently
        large benefit to the whole makes any sacrifice of
        individuals legitimate.
    </p>

    <p>
        At the same time, it does not claim that individual
        interests can never be limited to prevent greater
        and serious harm.
    </p>

    <p>
        Such decisions require attention to necessity,
        proportionality, alternatives, distribution of burdens,
        and the possibility of less destructive outcomes.
    </p>

    <p>
        Those who bear the greatest costs should not become
        invisible merely because their numbers are smaller
        or their interests are difficult to represent.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">LIMITS OF AGGREGATE BENEFIT</span>
        <p>
            The benefit of the whole does not automatically
            justify treating particular existences as expendable.
            Where serious sacrifices are proposed, their necessity,
            proportionality, alternatives, and distribution
            must be independently examined.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>44. The Coexistence Minimum</h3>

    <p>
        Although coexistence cannot guarantee that all interests
        will be satisfied, it requires certain minimum conditions
        if it is to remain more than the unrestricted domination
        of one existence by another.
    </p>

    <p>
        These conditions are not a complete theory of rights,
        nor do they assume that every form of existence can
        exercise them in the same way.
    </p>

    <p>
        They provide a minimum orientation for assessing
        serious asymmetries of influence:
    </p>

    <ul>
        <li><strong>Protection against arbitrary domination:</strong> significant power should remain limited and justifiable.</li>
        <li><strong>Consideration of affected interests:</strong> those affected should not be dismissed merely because they lack influence or representation.</li>
        <li><strong>Possibility of contestation:</strong> decisions should be open to challenge wherever meaningful participation is possible.</li>
        <li><strong>Protection of future possibilities:</strong> avoid unnecessary and irreversible foreclosure of meaningful development.</li>
        <li><strong>Recognition of independent existence:</strong> usefulness or cooperation should not be the sole basis for consideration.</li>
    </ul>

    <p>
        These conditions must be interpreted in context.
        Their implementation may differ between conscious
        individuals, non-human life, ecosystems, institutions,
        and systems whose interests or capacities remain
        uncertain.
    </p>

    <p>
        The inability to apply a condition in precisely the
        same way everywhere does not make the underlying
        concern irrelevant.
    </p>

    <h3>45. When Coexistence Cannot Be Preserved</h3>

    <p>
        There may be situations in which no available decision
        can preserve every legitimate interest or prevent
        every serious loss.
    </p>

    <p>
        A framework that refuses to acknowledge this possibility
        risks replacing moral reasoning with an impossible
        promise.
    </p>

    <p>
        When coexistence cannot be fully preserved, responsibility
        does not disappear. It becomes more demanding.
    </p>

    <p>
        The decision must be examined in light of what could
        reasonably have been known, what alternatives existed,
        who controlled the relevant conditions, and whether
        the burdens were imposed fairly.
    </p>

    <p>
        Where loss is unavoidable, it should be acknowledged
        rather than hidden behind abstract claims of necessity
        or progress.
    </p>

    <p>
        The framework does not promise that every tragedy
        can be prevented. It insists that unavoidable loss
        should not become an excuse for careless, arbitrary,
        or self-serving decisions.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">RESPONSIBILITY IN UNAVOIDABLE LOSS</span>
        <p>
            Where every available option involves serious loss,
            responsibility requires transparent justification,
            fair consideration of affected interests, examination
            of alternatives, and recognition of what cannot
            be restored.
        </p>
    </div>

    <h3>46. The Responsibility to Reconsider</h3>

    <p>
        A decision that was justified under one set of conditions
        may become unjustified when circumstances or knowledge
        change.
    </p>

    <p>
        Responsibility therefore does not end when a decision
        has been made.
    </p>

    <p>
        Significant decisions should remain open to reconsideration
        when new evidence emerges, consequences differ from
        what was anticipated, or the interests of affected
        existences become better understood.
    </p>

    <p>
        This is particularly important where decisions affect
        long periods of time, future generations, ecological
        conditions, or entities unable to challenge decisions
        directly.
    </p>

    <p>
        The ability to correct an earlier judgement is not
        necessarily evidence that the original decision was
        irresponsible. Refusing to reconsider it despite
        meaningful new evidence may be.
    </p>

    <div class="principle">
        <span class="principle-label">CONTINUOUS RECONSIDERATION</span>
        <p>
            Responsibility continues after a decision. Where
            new knowledge or consequences emerge, significant
            decisions should remain open to review and correction.
        </p>
    </div>

    <p>
        These principles establish the conditions under which
        conflicting claims may be assessed. The final part
        turns to their practical application: how answerability
        can be organised, how the framework can be challenged,
        and what would count as evidence that its principles
        need revision.
    </p>
</section>

<!-- END OF PART IV -->
